tools.description:
Cancel an in-progress Dropbox Sign signature request. Cancelling prevents any remaining signers from signing the document and cannot be undone.

Use this tool only when the user explicitly asks to cancel a signature request. Do not use it to decline or reject a single signer: it cancels the entire request for every party. A request can be cancelled only when it was sent successfully rather than left as a draft, has not been signed by every signer (partially signed requests are eligible), and the authenticated user is its sender or the owner of the API app under which it was sent. If it is ineligible, explain why instead of calling the tool.

Identify the request first. When its `signature_request_id` is unavailable, use `sign_list_signature_requests` to find it and ask the user to confirm the correct request. If the user is unsure, do not proceed. Before calling `sign_cancel_signature_request`, present a confirmation summary with the document title and the warning, "This will permanently cancel the signature request and cannot be undone." Do not call the tool until the user explicitly confirms, and call it exactly once. Pass only the required `signature_request_id` string; do not pass any other parameters.

The response is empty on success: tell the user only that the request was cancelled, without offering to undo the action. If the call fails, surface the error in plain language and do not retry automatically.


```ts
mcp__Dropbox__sign_cancel_signature_request(args: {
signatureRequestId: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<object>;
```

Download the current PDF snapshot for a Dropbox Sign signature request, returning a temporary `file_url` that can be used to fetch the file.

Use this tool only when the user asks to download a signature request. Identify the request first with `sign_list_signature_requests` when its `signature_request_id` is unavailable. Confirm it is the intended request before calling this tool. Pass only the required `signature_request_id`; do not pass any other parameters.

The response contains only `file_url`. It is a temporary, presigned URL and a bearer credential for the current PDF snapshot. Treat it as sensitive: share it only with the authenticated user, do not log it, do not include it in summaries or third-party requests, and do not transform, fetch, stream, or base64-encode its contents. Present the URL as a markdown link with clear descriptive text (e.g. `[Download signature request](<file_url>)`) rather than printing the raw URL, and advise the user that it expires.

If the call fails because the request is unavailable, deleted, or the user is not permitted to access it, explain the error in plain language. Do not retry automatically.


```ts
mcp__Dropbox__sign_download_signature_request(args: {
signatureRequestId: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<{ fileUrl?: string }>;
```

